    slug: "google-nest-hub-2nd-gen-smart-display",
    description: "The 7-inch smart display category is split between Amazon's Echo Show 8 and Google's own Nest lineup, and the second-generation Nest Hub lands at the smaller, more accessible end of that spectrum — below the Nest Hub Max's 10-inch footprint and camera. What sets this generation apart from the original isn't display or audio improvements; it's the addition of a Soli radar chip, the same millimeter-wave sensing technology Google first deployed in the Pixel 4. That hardware choice is the entire design thesis of the product, positioning it as much as a bedside health device as a kitchen assistant or home control panel.\n\nThe 7-inch touchscreen runs at 1024x600, which is adequate for ambient content and recipe cards but noticeably soft next to the Echo Show 8's 1280x800 IPS panel. Color calibration skews warm by default, tuned for bedroom ambient light rather than video accuracy, and the display adapts brightness using an ambient light sensor with reasonable granularity. The 1.7-inch full-range driver is a single-speaker system — no tweeter, no passive radiator — which means audio tops out around 70–75dB with rolled-off highs and thin bass response. Don't use this as a primary music speaker; the Nest Audio or even a paired Bluetooth device will outperform it substantially. Wi-Fi tops out at 802.11ac (Wi-Fi 5), which is a real limitation in 2024 — no Wi-Fi 6 support means higher latency on congested networks. Bluetooth 5.0 handles device pairing reliably. The Soli chip operates at 60GHz, detecting breathing rate, movement, and sleep stage transitions without any wearable or contact requirement.\n\nIn practice, the sleep tracking is the headline differentiator and it earns its place. Soli detects respiratory rate within a range of roughly 0.5m–1.5m from the device, and Google's Sleep Sensing dashboard in the Home app surfaces sleep onset time, disturbance events, and cough and snore detection — though the premium features now sit behind a Fitbit Premium subscription, which is a meaningful ongoing cost to factor in. The radar also powers Motion Sense gestures for snooze and media skip without screen touch, which works consistently within about 30cm. Thermal behavior is a non-issue for a passively cooled ambient device. The Google Assistant integration handles smart home control well — particularly with Matter and Google Home ecosystem devices — though local processing is limited and cloud dependency is real. Response latency over a solid Wi-Fi connection sits around 1–2 seconds for most queries.\n\nThe right buyer is someone already inside the Google ecosystem who wants a bedside or kitchen ambient display with passive health monitoring — not someone building a serious audio zone or expecting tablet-grade media playback. Against the Echo Show 8, the Nest Hub wins on Assistant/smart home integration depth and the unique Soli-based sleep tracking, but loses on display resolution, stereo audio, and the lack of a camera for video calling. At $108, it undercuts the Echo Show 8 at list price, though Amazon's frequent discounting closes that gap. Skip up to the Nest Hub Max if video calling or louder audio matters; stay here if the radar sleep tracking is the actual draw.",
