    title: `Ring Video Doorbell 4 with Enhanced Wi-Fi`,
    slug: "ring-video-doorbell-4-with-enhanced-wi-fi",
    description: "Battery-powered video doorbells occupy a crowded middle tier between wired hardwired units and cheap Wi-Fi cameras, and the fourth-generation Ring sits squarely in the mainstream of that category — not the flagship (that's the Ring Video Doorbell Pro 2 with its 1536p head-to-toe frame and radar-based package detection), but a genuine step above entry-level. The headline hardware addition over its predecessor is Color Pre-Roll, a feature that distinguishes this unit from most competitors at the price. The quick-release battery pack is a practical choice for renters and retrofit installs where cutting into existing wiring isn't viable.\n\nThe 1080p sensor delivers a 160° horizontal by 84° vertical field of view — wide enough to capture approaching visitors at distance but with a notably narrower vertical angle than the Pro 2's near-square aspect ratio, which means dropped packages at your feet are a real edge case for the crop. Color Pre-Roll is the more technically interesting spec here: the camera continuously buffers four seconds of color footage before a motion trigger, using a low-power capture mode to write ambient frames to memory. Where the previous generation only offered black-and-white pre-roll, color context meaningfully changes what you can identify — a car color, clothing — before the main event clip even starts. Connectivity is limited to 2.4 GHz 802.11 b/g/n, which means no 5 GHz band and no Wi-Fi 5 or 6 support. At this price point in 2024, that's a real constraint — throughput ceiling sits around 54–72 Mbps theoretical, and in RF-congested environments (dense apartment buildings, urban installs) you'll feel it in reconnect latency and occasional dropped motion clips.\n\nIn daily use, the battery chemistry and quick-release design are the practical centerpiece. Ring doesn't publish official rated battery life in hours, but real-world reports from moderate-traffic installs average 6–12 weeks per charge depending on motion event frequency — high-traffic front doors (20+ events daily) will push users toward monthly charging cycles. The quick-release mechanism means a swap takes under 60 seconds with a spare pack, which is the right answer if you don't want to wire in a trickle charge. Night vision defaults to infrared with color available in low-light conditions rather than total darkness, so don't expect true color performance without ambient street or porch lighting. The 2.4 GHz-only limitation also affects latency: live view open times on Ring's app average 5–8 seconds on a clear network, noticeably slower than wired competitors like the Nest Doorbell (wired) or the Arlo Video Doorbell 2K. Ring Protect subscription unlocks cloud history and package alerts — without it, you're limited to live view and real-time notifications only.\n\nThe right buyer here is a renter or homeowner who wants a meaningful upgrade from a first-generation Ring or a budget Wyze Doorbell without committing to hardwired installation or a premium price. The Color Pre-Roll feature alone justifies the step up from the Ring Video Doorbell (2022) at $169, where pre-roll is black-and-white only. Buyers prioritizing resolution or package detection should step up to the Pro 2 at $279, or consider the Nest Doorbell (battery) at $179 for Google Home ecosystem integration. At $219.99, the fourth-generation Ring beats most of the battery-powered field on software maturity and ecosystem depth, but it doesn't beat the category on raw video specs or connectivity standards.",
