    title: `iRobot Roomba j7+ Self-Emptying Robot Vacuum`,
    slug: "irobot-roomba-j7-self-emptying-robot-vacuum",
    description: "The j7+ sits at the upper tier of the consumer robot vacuum market — call it the $700–900 bracket where Roborock's S7 MaxV Ultra and Ecovacs' DEEBOT X1 OMNI set the reference points. iRobot's entry here is notable not for raw suction figures but for its camera-based navigation stack and obstacle avoidance logic, which is a deliberate architectural choice over the LiDAR-plus-structured-light approach Roborock and Ecovacs favor. The self-emptying base is a 60-day-capacity bag system, meaning the Clean Base handles debris consolidation without requiring constant user intervention — a meaningful quality-of-life decision for the target household.\n\nPrecisionVision Navigation uses a forward-facing RGB camera combined on-device with iRobot's object recognition model, which is where the P.O.O.P. (Pet Owner Official Promise) feature lives. That's not marketing fluff — it represents a trained classifier specifically tuned to detect pet waste, cables, socks, and similar floor-level hazards that pure LiDAR units routinely destroy or redistribute. The tradeoff is that camera-based nav is lighting-dependent in a way LiDAR isn't; low-light performance degrades, and the j7+ is slower to build initial room maps than a dual-sensor LiDAR unit. Suction is rated at 10x relative to the 600 series, which iRobot pegs at roughly 1,700–2,000 Pa in third-party bench tests — competitive with mid-range Roborock S7 variants but behind the 5,000+ Pa figures the X1 OMNI and S8 MaxV Ultra advertise. Runtime caps at 75 minutes, which is below the 90–120 minutes common at this price.\n\nIn daily use, the 75-minute runtime is the sharpest practical constraint. On a 1,500–2,000 sq ft single-floor home with mixed hard floors and medium-pile carpet, expect one or two recharge-and-resume cycles per full clean. The Clean Base auto-empty mechanism is audible — roughly 10 seconds of high-pitched suction at around 70 dB — so scheduled overnight runs are worth configuring. Wi-Fi connectivity is 2.4 GHz only, which means no 5 GHz band option and some latency variance on congested networks. The iRobot Home app supports both Alexa and Google Assistant integration with reasonable voice command coverage. No mopping module is available for the j7+, which is a genuine gap versus the Roborock S8 MaxV Ultra and DEEBOT X1 OMNI at comparable pricing. Where it punches above its bracket is obstacle avoidance reliability — the vision system genuinely earns its keep in households with dogs or cable-heavy desk setups.\n\nThe j7+ is the right call for pet owners, specifically multi-dog or multi-cat households where floor hazard density is high enough that a LiDAR-only unit would require constant pre-run tidying. It's also well-suited to users who want a set-and-forget experience without babysitting the machine or pre-clearing rooms. Buyers who want mopping alongside vacuuming should move to the Roborock S8 MaxV Ultra or DEEBOT X1 OMNI, both of which offer combined wet-dry operation at similar price points. At $799, the j7+ beats the competition on obstacle avoidance confidence and brand software maturity, but concedes on raw suction, runtime, and feature breadth.",
