    title: `TP-Link Kasa Smart Plug Mini (4-Pack)`,
    slug: "tp-link-kasa-smart-plug-mini-4-pack",
    description: "At $6.50 per outlet, the Kasa EP25 Mini sits in the densest part of the smart plug market — competing directly with the Amazon Smart Plug, Wyze Plug Compact, and Tapo P125M. What separates the Kasa lineup from budget white-label alternatives is TP-Link's relatively mature cloud infrastructure and local LAN control capability, which matters more than most buyers realize until their internet goes down. The form factor is legitimately compact at 2.6\" x 1.5\" x 1.5\", meaning it occupies a single outlet on a standard duplex receptacle without blocking the second — a bar that several competitors, including older Kasa models, fail to clear.\n\nThe 15A / 1800W maximum load rating puts this at the practical ceiling for a standard 15A residential circuit, which means it handles essentially any appliance you'd realistically plug into a wall outlet short of a 20A-circuit device like a window AC or specific power tools. Connectivity is 2.4 GHz 802.11 b/g/n only — no 5 GHz, no Wi-Fi 6, no Thread or Matter support in this SKU. That's a deliberate cost-down, and it means the plug won't natively participate in a Matter fabric without a bridge. The onboard energy monitoring — tracking wattage draw, voltage, and cumulative kWh — logs through the Kasa app and gives usable per-device consumption data, which is functionally more granular than what the Amazon Smart Plug offers at a comparable price point. There's no local API baked in from the factory, but the Kasa protocol is well-documented, and Home Assistant integration via the tplink integration is stable.\n\nIn daily operation, response latency over cloud is typically 300–800ms — acceptable for scheduling and voice control, but noticeable if you're triggering automations that need sub-second precision. Local LAN polling drops that to under 100ms for Home Assistant users. The Kasa app handles geofencing, schedules, and scenes without a subscription, which is increasingly rare. No Zigbee, no Z-Wave, no Thread — this is a pure Wi-Fi device, so dense deployments across a home with a congested 2.4 GHz band may see reliability issues. The trade-off for skipping Matter is the simplicity: setup is two taps in the Kasa app or standard Alexa / Google Home discovery, and it works without a hub. Energy monitoring accuracy is within roughly 2–3% based on community testing against a Kill-A-Watt, which is adequate for usage tracking but not billing-grade precision.\n\nThe right buyer is the homeowner building out a basic smart home on a budget — someone who wants scheduled outlets, voice control, and basic energy visibility without committing to a Zigbee or Matter ecosystem yet. At $6.50 per plug with energy monitoring included, it undercuts the Meross MSS305 and outperforms the Amazon Smart Plug on feature depth. Skip this in favor of the Tapo P125M if Matter fabric integration is a priority, or the Eve Energy if HomeKit and Thread are central to the setup. For anyone already running Alexa or Google Home with a stable 2.4 GHz network and no plans to migrate ecosystems, the four-pack is a practical bulk buy with real utility.",
    price: 25.98,
    category: "Smart Home",
    categorySlug: "smart-home",
