    id: 8,
    title: `Arlo Pro 5S 2K Spotlight Security Camera`,
    slug: "arlo-pro-5s-2k-spotlight-security-camera",
    description: "The Pro 5S sits in the upper-mid tier of battery-powered outdoor security cameras — above Wyze and Eufy's budget lines, below Arlo's own Ultra 2 and the wired-first competition like Reolink's Argus series. At $169.99, it's priced against the Google Nest Cam (battery) and the Ring Spotlight Cam Plus, and Arlo is betting on resolution and ecosystem maturity to justify the premium over Ring's $149 ask. The 2K HDR output is a meaningful differentiator here: at 2560×1440, it clears the threshold where license plates and facial detail hold up after digital zoom, which 1080p cameras routinely fail on at distances beyond 15 feet.\n\nThe 2K HDR pipeline is doing real work — HDR processing in a battery-powered camera typically means frame blending or tone-mapping rather than true dual-exposure, so expect well-handled highlight recovery in mixed-light scenes rather than the high-dynamic-range latitude you'd get from a wired unit with a larger sensor. The 160° diagonal field of view is wider than the Nest Cam battery's 130° and Ring's 140°, which meaningfully reduces blind spots at entry points without requiring digital distortion correction that eats resolution. Color night vision relies on the onboard spotlight rather than a dedicated low-light sensor pathway — sensitivity in ambient-only conditions will be limited, so the LED is doing the heavy lifting. Dual-band Wi-Fi support (2.4 and 5GHz) matters for placement flexibility; 5GHz keeps latency low if the camera is within range, though 2.4GHz will be the practical choice for anything mounted far from a router. Bluetooth is present for initial pairing, not for ongoing data.\n\nClaimed 8-month battery life is measured under Arlo's standard test conditions — roughly 5 motion-triggered clips per day. Expect 3 to 4 months under heavier traffic (10–15 clips daily) or in cold climates where lithium cell capacity drops noticeably below 0°C. The IP65 rating means full dust ingress protection and water resistance against low-pressure jets from any direction, which covers rain and hose spray but not submersion — appropriate for any exposed outdoor mount. Arlo's ecosystem still pushes hard toward the Arlo Secure subscription for person, vehicle, and package detection; without it, you're limited to motion alerts and local storage via a separately purchased SmartHub. Cloud storage free tier is 30 days on plan, nothing off plan. No local RTSP stream is available natively, which will frustrate Home Assistant users and anyone who wants NVR integration without workarounds.\n\nThe right buyer here is someone already in or committed to Arlo's ecosystem who needs a wire-free outdoor camera with enough resolution to be forensically useful. The 160° FOV and 2K output make it stronger than the Ring Spotlight Cam Plus for detail capture at equivalent price. Skip it for the Arlo Ultra 2 if 4K output and a built-in siren matter — the Ultra 2 runs $50 to $80 more but adds wire-free flexibility with a substantial sensor upgrade. At $169.99, it beats the Nest Cam battery on resolution and field of view, but loses on the Google Home integration depth and the Nest's cleaner no-subscription local storage story.",
    price: 169.99,
