    id: 34,
    title: `Elgato HD60 X External Capture Card`,
    slug: "elgato-hd60-x-external-capture-card",
    description: "The HD60 X sits in the mid-tier external capture card segment, competing directly with AVerMedia's Live Gamer Portable 2 Plus and the Razer Ripsaw HD, while undercutting the AVerMedia 4K Pro Capture and Elgato's own 4K X by a significant margin. The key hardware differentiator here is passthrough fidelity: the unit handles 4K30 and 1080p60 with HDR10 on the passthrough chain, and critically, it supports Variable Refresh Rate in passthrough — a spec most cards at this price strip out. The USB 3.0 Type-C interface keeps the setup tool-free with no PCIe slot required.\n\nThe capture pipeline tops out at 4K30 or 1080p60, both with HDR10 — the HDR10 support is genuinely useful for PS5 and Xbox Series X users who run HDR output and don't want to toggle between capture and play modes. The onboard H.264/H.265 hardware encoding offloads compression from the host CPU, which matters if the streaming rig is also running OBS with scene transitions, browser sources, and a heavy plugin stack. H.265 at this tier means smaller file sizes at equivalent quality versus H.264 — practical for long session VOD storage. VRR passthrough support means the display connected to the HDMI out loop won't drop to fixed refresh when the card is in the chain, preserving 120Hz+ VRR performance on Xbox Series X or PS5 in supported titles. The USB 3.0 interface provides roughly 5 Gbps bandwidth — sufficient for the capture bitrates involved but a ceiling that rules out uncompressed 4K60 capture, which the card doesn't attempt anyway.\n\nIn daily use, the near-zero latency passthrough claim holds up — the loop-through path is hardware-direct and adds no perceptible delay, so the monitor signal is clean for competitive play. Capture latency to the host software is a separate figure, running around 70–100ms in typical OBS configurations, which is standard for USB-class cards and irrelevant for most streaming workflows. The USB 3.0 bus means plug-and-play on any modern laptop or desktop without PCIe real estate concerns, though USB-C to USB-A adapter chains can introduce instability worth avoiding. 4K60 capture is absent — that ceiling sits at 4K30 — which is the clearest place this product trails the AVerMedia 4K Pro or Elgato 4K X. Audio passthrough is handled digitally via HDMI; there's no analog 3.5mm routing, so console headset audio mixed through the HDMI chain works, but analog sources don't. Windows and macOS support is solid; 4K Capture Utility and OBS plugin integration are mature.\n\nThe target buyer is a console streamer running a PS5 or Xbox Series X through an existing PC or Mac setup who wants HDR and VRR passthrough without paying flagship card prices. It makes more sense here than the AVerMedia Live Gamer Portable 2 Plus if HDR10 accuracy on the monitor loop matters, or if the source console outputs VRR and the user wants to preserve that. Skip it for the Elgato 4K X if 4K60 capture is a real workflow requirement — that extra 30 frames is meaningful for high-frame-rate console footage. At $199.99, the HD60 X beats most of the competition on passthrough feature density but doesn't challenge dedicated PCIe cards on raw capture throughput.",
    price: 199.99,
