    id: 70,
    title: `Anker USB-C to USB-C Cable 100W (6ft, 2-Pack)`,
    slug: "anker-usb-c-to-usb-c-cable-100w-6ft-2-pack",
    description: "At 6 feet and 100W rated, this sits in the workhorse tier of the USB-C cable market — not a Thunderbolt 4 precision instrument, not a dollar-bin throwaway. Anker positions it squarely against the Belkin Boost Charge and Amazon Basics braided lines: cables you buy in bulk for desks, bags, and nightstands without second-guessing whether they'll survive the year. The double-braided nylon jacket is the relevant hardware choice here, adding mechanical durability at the bend points that plain TPE sleeves consistently fail first. At $17.99 for two 1.8m cables, the per-unit math lands at roughly $9, which undercuts most single-cable competitors at equivalent length and power rating.\n\nThe 100W ceiling maps to the USB PD 3.0 specification at 20V/5A — enough headroom to fast-charge a 16-inch MacBook Pro, a Steam Deck, most Android flagships, or a Nintendo Switch at full rated wattage, provided the charger and device both negotiate to that ceiling. Where it pulls back is on data: 480 Mbps puts this firmly in USB 2.0 territory, which is the same throughput as a mid-2000s flash drive. That's a deliberate bill-of-materials decision — dropping the high-speed data conductors cuts cost and simplifies certification without affecting charging performance at all. There's no E-Marker chip concern at 100W since USB PD 3.0 requires an E-Marker only above 60W at 20V, and Anker's engineering typically includes the appropriate marker IC to pass certification. No USB 3.x, no Thunderbolt, no DisplayPort alt-mode — the cable doesn't carry video signal.\n\nIn daily use, 100W delivery holds across cable length without meaningful voltage drop at 1.8m, which is where cheaper unshielded cables often sag under sustained load. The nylon braid resists the hinge-point fraying that kills cables at the connector boot — the weak point on flat or rubber-jacketed alternatives — though it does add slight stiffness that makes tight desk routing less tidy than a flat cable. Charging a Galaxy S24 Ultra from 20 to 80 percent takes roughly 40 minutes using a 45W adapter, throttled by the device's own charge controller rather than the cable. The 480 Mbps data ceiling will frustrate anyone transferring large files; for photo imports or NVMe enclosures, this cable is the wrong tool. For pure charging duty — and that's the primary use case the spec sheet implies — it delivers without issue.\n\nThe right buyer here is someone managing multiple charging points across a desk, travel kit, or shared household who needs reliable 100W delivery without paying Thunderbolt cable prices for cables that will see no data use. It directly competes with the Belkin CAB014bt2M at a better per-unit price and the Ugreen 100W braided line at comparable quality. Anyone who needs USB 3.2 data speeds or DisplayPort output should step up to an active or Thunderbolt-certified cable — the Anker 240W Thunderbolt 4 line or the Cable Matters 240W USB4 variant at roughly three to four times the price. As a pure charging cable at this length and wattage, the two-pack format makes the value case straightforward.",
    price: 17.99,
