    title: "Cricut Joy 2 Compact Cutting Machine",
    slug: "cricut-joy-2-compact-cutting-machine",
    description: "The Cricut Joy 2 sits at the entry-to-mid tier of the consumer vinyl cutter and craft plotter market — a category that also includes the Silhouette Cameo 4 Small, the Cricut Explore 4, and Brother's ScanNCut lineup. At $99, it undercuts the Explore 4 by a significant margin while targeting users who don't need the Explore's 12-inch cutting width or dual-tool carriage. The 4.5-inch cutting width is the defining hardware constraint here, and whether it's a dealbreaker or a non-issue entirely depends on the project scope. The Bluetooth-only connectivity keeps the form factor compact and cable-free, but rules out any USB fallback for environments with wireless interference.\n\nThe 4.5-inch maximum cut width — 11.4cm — is narrow by plotter standards, but the Joy 2's strength is its support for over 75 compatible materials, which includes vinyl, iron-on (HTV), cardstock, writable labels, and foil transfer sheets. Three tool modes — cutting, writing/drawing, and scoring/foiling — cover the practical range of what most label-and-sticker users need. The Print Then Cut feature is the meaningful spec upgrade from base Joy functionality: it allows the machine to register and cut around full-color printed artwork, enabling custom sticker production that would otherwise require scissors or a more expensive plotter. Accuracy on Print Then Cut relies on sensor registration marks, and at this price tier the registration tolerance is tighter than you'd get from a manual cut but looser than a dedicated label cutter like a Graphtec CE7000. Design Space runs on iOS, Android, macOS, and Windows, with cloud-based file storage — which means offline use is limited.\n\nIn daily use, the Joy 2's Bluetooth connection introduces occasional pairing latency on first connect, but cut jobs stream reliably once linked. Cut speed is adequate for small batches — think a sheet of custom stickers or a run of vinyl labels — but sustained longer jobs will expose the narrower throat size as a real workflow bottleneck compared to the Cameo 4 Small or Explore 4. Print Then Cut alignment is consistent within roughly ±0.5mm for most home printer outputs, provided you're using a printer with accurate color registration. The free Design Space app is functional but cloud-dependent, and the subscription-free SVG import path works well for users bringing their own assets. There's no offline mode for cutting without an internet connection on initial setup. Material compatibility at 75+ is broad on paper, but thicker materials like balsa or chipboard are at the edge of what the Joy 2's motor can handle without multiple passes.\n\nThe Joy 2 fits best with apartment-dwellers, small-business sellers making custom stickers or labels in short runs, and anyone who wants a dedicated plotter without dedicating a full desk to it. The compact footprint makes it a practical shelf item in a way the Cameo 4 or Explore 4 aren't. Buyers who regularly cut 12-inch vinyl sheets, work with fabric frequently, or want dual-tool simultaneous operation should step up to the Explore 4 or the Cameo 4 Small — both offer meaningfully more headroom for about $80–120 more. At $99, the Joy 2 beats the Silhouette Portrait 4 on material compatibility and the Print Then Cut feature, though the Portrait edges it on cut width at 8 inches. Value is solid for the defined use case; outside it, the 4.5-inch constraint shows quickly.",
