    id: 79,
    title: "LEGO Editions Cristiano Ronaldo – Soccer Highlights",
    slug: "lego-editions-cristiano-ronaldo-soccer-highlights",
    description: "The LEGO Editions line for licensed athlete figures sits in a niche between LEGO's own Icons series and its more affordable Creator sets — premium enough to justify display shelf placement, accessible enough to serve as an entry point for younger builders. At $29.99 and 490 pieces, model 43012 lands at roughly $0.06 per piece, which is competitive for a licensed IP set at this part count. The Ronaldo theming puts it squarely against comparable athlete and celebrity LEGO releases in the collectibles space, where the build itself is secondary to the subject matter and the display value of the finished piece.\n\nThe 490-piece count suggests a mid-complexity build — more involved than a polybag or impulse set, but well short of the 1,000-plus piece flagship LEGO sets that define the Icons and Technic upper tiers. Targeting ages 10 and up aligns with LEGO's standard rubric for sets that involve repetitive stud-and-plate layering, some interior structural work, and possibly a minifigure or mosaic-style portrait component, though without confirmed set imagery that's an inference from part count and category. The educational focus listed — fine motor skills, sensory development, creative thinking — is boilerplate for LEGO at this tier, but the fine motor and sequencing challenge is genuine for the age range, particularly in any brick-built figure construction requiring precise color placement. ABS plastic construction is standard LEGO spec: dimensionally consistent, clutch-power stable over time, and colorfast under normal display conditions.\n\nIn real-world terms, a 490-piece set in this category typically runs 90 to 150 minutes for a focused builder in the target age range, less for experienced adult fans of LEGO (AFOL). The \"Soccer Highlights\" naming implies interactive or scene-based elements rather than a pure static display piece — likely a diorama or vignette format with a playable field section and a poseable or minifigure-scale Ronaldo representation. If that's accurate, durability under repeated handling matters, and ABS at standard LEGO tolerances holds well there. The trade-off versus higher-end mosaic sets like the 1,000-plus piece Art series is significant: at 490 pieces, the resolution on any portrait or detailed figure will be noticeably coarser, which is the honest ceiling of what this price tier delivers.\n\nThe right buyer here is a Ronaldo fan aged 10 to 14 who builds regularly and wants something displayable that also has play value, or a casual adult collector filling out a sports memorabilia shelf without committing to a flagship LEGO price point. At $29.99 it competes directly with other licensed sports sets from LEGO and third-party brick brands like Mega. Anyone prioritizing build complexity or display resolution over the IP itself would be better served by stepping up to LEGO Icons or Art. For the Ronaldo household, this hits the right price-to-purpose ratio.",
