    id: 118,
    title: "Amazon Echo Studio (2025) Spatial Smart Speaker",
    slug: "amazon-echo-studio-2025-spatial-smart-speaker",
    description: "The Echo Studio has occupied a specific niche since 2019: the only mainstream spatial audio speaker under $300 that doesn't require a paired soundbar or receiver. The 2025 revision keeps that positioning intact while upgrading the processing muscle. At $220, it sits above the Sonos Era 100 ($249, though frequently discounted) in spatial ambition but below the Sonos Era 300 ($449) in driver count and room-filling headroom. The key hardware refresh here is the AZ3 Pro chip, Amazon's latest neural audio processor, which replaces the AZ2 found in the previous generation and handles the real-time upmixing and spatial rendering that defines the speaker's core identity.\n\nThe driver array — three 1.5-inch full-range units arranged for left, right, and upward firing, plus a 3.75-inch downward-firing woofer — is carried over in configuration from the prior generation, but the AZ3 Pro changes what that hardware can actually do. The previous model relied on Dolby Atmos and Sony 360 Reality Audio passthrough with limited dynamic processing headroom; the new chip enables more aggressive real-time upmixing of stereo and standard 5.1 content into height-channel simulation, which is where most streaming libraries still live. The woofer is ported to the bottom of the enclosure, using the floor as a bass reflector — effective in practice but placement-sensitive. The speaker's effective bass reproduction is credible down to around 35Hz in-room, which is competitive for its footprint. The AZ3 Pro also runs the local Alexa+ inference stack, enabling on-device voice processing that reduces round-trip latency for smart home commands compared to cloud-dependent architectures.\n\nThe Echo Studio supports Wi-Fi 6 multi-room audio sync via Amazon's ecosystem and Bluetooth for direct device pairing, though the Bluetooth implementation remains a meaningful limitation — there's no LDAC, no aptX HD, no LHDC. You're limited to SBC and AAC over Bluetooth, which caps wireless hi-res audio at the codec rather than the source. For spatial audio, you'll want it streaming directly from a service like Amazon Music HD or Apple Music via Wi-Fi rather than routing through Bluetooth from a phone. The built-in Matter and Zigbee hub is genuinely useful — it removes a separate hub from the chain for Zigbee device control — and the dual-band Wi-Fi handles multi-room sync reliably at low latency. Thermal behavior isn't a concern at this power class. The noise floor is low enough for near-field listening. Sustained loud playback does show mild dynamic compression above 80% volume.\n\nThe right buyer here is someone deep in Amazon's ecosystem — Prime Music HD subscriber, Alexa+ household, existing Echo multi-room setup — who wants spatial audio without running a receiver-based system. The spatial upmixing is the genuine differentiator versus the Sonos Era 100 at a similar street price; the Era 100 is a better stereo speaker, but the Echo Studio does something the Era 100 cannot. Buyers who stream primarily from Spotify or use Bluetooth as their main input will leave most of the hardware's capability on the table. The Era 300 is the step up for anyone who wants true multichannel driver placement rather than simulated height channels. At $220, the Echo Studio beats everything in its price range on spatial audio ambition; it doesn't beat the Era 100 on stereo fidelity or the HomePod (2nd gen) on tonal accuracy.",
