    id: 130,
    title: "DJI Osmo Pocket 4 Gimbal Camera",
    slug: "dji-osmo-pocket-4-gimbal-camera",
    description: "The Osmo Pocket line has always occupied a specific and contested niche: pocket-sized 3-axis mechanical gimbal cameras aimed at run-and-gun creators who want stabilized footage without the bulk of a mirrorless rig or the rolling-shutter punishment of an action cam. The fourth generation pushes into genuinely serious imaging territory with a 1-inch CMOS sensor — the same format class as Sony's RX100 VII and the DJI Air 3's primary camera — paired with an f/2.0 fixed aperture. That combination puts it well above the 1/1.7-inch and 1/2-inch sensors that dominated previous Pocket generations, and it directly challenges the Sony ZV-1 II and Canon PowerShot V10 as a compact daily shooter.\n\nThe 1-inch sensor at f/2.0 delivers meaningful low-light headroom — roughly two stops over a 1/1.7-inch equivalent — and the 14-stop dynamic range figure is competitive with APS-C cameras in controlled conditions, though real-world DR depends heavily on how DJI meters and tone-maps internally. The 10-bit D-Log color profile is the critical spec here: it gives colorists genuine latitude in post, with enough bit depth to avoid banding in graduated skies or skin tone grading. 4K at 240fps is an extraordinary headline number for this sensor size; sustaining that framerate at 4K likely involves a crop factor and aggressive compression, so users should verify the actual field of view and bitrate before assuming it matches 4K/60 quality. The 800 MB/s internal transfer speed over what is presumably a USB 3.2 or UHS-II-class interface means offloading 107 GB of built-in storage takes under three minutes — a genuine workflow benefit versus the Pocket 3's SD card dependency.\n\nIn daily use, the mechanical gimbal remains the product's core advantage over optical or electronic stabilization — it handles low-frequency walking motion that EIS alone smears or crops around. The f/2.0 aperture is fixed, so exposure control in bright conditions relies entirely on ND filters (likely sold separately, consistent with DJI's accessory model). Thermal behavior at 4K/240fps sustained recording will be worth watching; compact bodies with 1-inch sensors have historically throttled under extended high-framerate loads, as seen in early Sony ZV-E1 and RX100-series use cases. Bluetooth and Wi-Fi connectivity for the companion app are expected, though codec support for external monitoring and audio — whether the mic array targets cardioid or omnidirectional polar patterns, and whether a 3.5mm or USB-C audio input is present — remains unconfirmed from available specs.\n\nThe right buyer here is a travel documentarian or social creator who shoots in varied lighting, edits in DaVinci Resolve or Premiere, and needs footage that holds up in color grading without hauling a gimbal-plus-mirrorless kit. At $497, it undercuts a Sony ZV-1 II plus external gimbal combination by a meaningful margin while delivering a larger sensor and internal 10-bit log. Mobile-first shooters happy with smartphone footage and EIS should look elsewhere — the mechanical gimbal form factor adds physical bulk that only pays off when footage quality is a priority. Those needing interchangeable lenses or full manual control should step up to an OM System OM-5 or Sony ZV-E10 II kit instead.",
    price: 497,
    category: "Everyday Carry & Accessories",
