    id: 138,
    title: "CORSAIR 3200D RS ARGB: Mid-Tower PC Case",
    slug: "corsair-3200d-rs-argb-mid-tower-pc-case",
    description: "The 3200D RS ARGB sits in the sub-$100 mid-tower segment, which is one of the most competitive price brackets in PC cases right now. It goes up against the Fractal Pop Air, the Montech Sky Two, and Phanteks' Eclipse G360A — all fighting over builders who want airflow, RGB, and tempered glass without crossing into premium territory. The ATX mid-tower form factor is broadly compatible, and Corsair's decision to ship it with three pre-installed RS120 ARGB fans rather than leaving the buyer to source their own is the headline hardware choice here. That alone meaningfully distinguishes it from bare-bones competitors at the same price.\n\nThe 360 mm radiator support on both the front and roof panels gives this case genuine AIO flexibility — a 360 mm cooler on the front paired with exhaust potential on the roof means builders aren't immediately capacity-constrained when upgrading cooling. The GPU clearance at 375 mm handles the full-length RTX 4080 Super and RX 7900 GRE without issue, though cards pushing beyond that (some 4090 triple-slot designs) will need measurement checks. Fan mounting tops out at nine 120 mm positions or four 140 mm, which is more ceiling than most buyers in this bracket will ever use, but it means the case doesn't artificially constrain airflow configurations. The RS120 fans included are Corsair's budget ARGB line — they move reasonable air and address iCUE if you're in the Corsair ecosystem, but they aren't the SP120 Elite tier in terms of static pressure or noise-per-CFM. Storage is modest at three 2.5-inch and one 3.5-inch bay, which suits most modern SSD-primary builds but limits anyone still running large HDD arrays.\n\nIn daily use, the front I/O spec mix is worth noting: the USB 3.2 Gen 2x2 Type-C port delivers up to 20 Gbps, which is faster than what many motherboards in this price range actually expose on the front-panel header — verify motherboard compatibility before treating that as a given. The USB 2.0 Type-A alongside it is the visible cost-cut versus cases offering dual USB 3.2 Gen 1 Type-A. Tempered glass on the side panel is standard at this price but the RS line's panel mounting tends toward a simpler hinge-free design, which affects how easily you get inside. Thermal behavior will lean heavily on how those three included intake fans are configured versus rear exhaust — the case doesn't include a rear fan, so that's an additional $15–25 spend for a complete push-pull or intake-dominant setup.\n\nThe right buyer here is someone building a mid-range gaming or workstation PC — Ryzen 7 or Core i5/i7 class, discrete GPU in the RTX 4070 tier — who wants Corsair's iCUE ARGB integration without paying for the iCUE 4000X or 5000X. The three included fans remove a line item that competitors skip to hit the same price. Builders already invested in the Corsair ecosystem get the cleaner software experience; those on other RGB controllers will find the fans work as standard ARGB headers too. Skip this for the 4000D Airflow if budget stretches to $100-plus, because Corsair's own higher tier offers meaningfully better airflow geometry and superior fan quality — but at $79.99, the 3200D RS ARGB undercuts that without embarrassing itself.",
    price: 79.99,
    category: "Everyday Carry & Accessories",
    categorySlug: "everyday-carry",
