    id: 140,
    title: "MSI HERALD BE9400",
    slug: "msi-herald-be9400",
    description: "At $49.99, the HERALD BE9400 sits in the budget-to-mid tier of PCIe Wi-Fi adapters, a category currently being reshuffled by the arrival of affordable 802.11be silicon. The relevant competitive frame here is the Intel BE200 and MediaTek-based cards like the TP-Link Archer TXE75E — all chasing the same sub-$60 Wi-Fi 7 sweet spot. The BE9400 designation signals a theoretical aggregate throughput ceiling of around 9.4 Gbps, which places it above entry-level BE3600 cards but below the full BE19000 tri-band flagship tier. What makes the category interesting right now is that the chipset — almost certainly a MediaTek or Qualcomm BE solution — brings 320 MHz channel support to a price point where 160 MHz used to be the ceiling.\n\nThe tri-band configuration here is not symmetric. The 6 GHz band tops out at 5,764 Mbps using 320 MHz channel width with 4096-QAM modulation — that's the headline number and it requires a Wi-Fi 7 router with MLO and a clean 6 GHz channel to hit anything close. The 5 GHz radio delivers up to 2,882 Mbps, consistent with a 2x2 MIMO stream count at 160 MHz, and the 2.4 GHz band caps at 688 Mbps, which is standard for a 2x2 configuration at that frequency. Multi-Link Operation is the spec that actually matters in a Wi-Fi 7 card — it allows the adapter to bond across bands simultaneously rather than simply roaming between them, reducing effective latency and improving throughput stability under congestion. WPA3 is mandatory here, not a differentiator. The PCIe interface ensures the host-side bandwidth is never the bottleneck; even PCIe 3.0 x1 exceeds what the radio can deliver. Bluetooth 5.4 adds support for LE Audio and the LC3 codec architecture, though practical codec support depends on the host driver stack.\n\nIn real-world use, the 6 GHz performance numbers are ceiling figures requiring line-of-sight proximity to a BE router — through a wall or two, expect the card to fall back to 5 GHz where it still meaningfully outperforms Wi-Fi 6E adapters thanks to MLO aggregation. Latency under load is where Wi-Fi 7 earns its keep for desktop use: MLO can push gaming ping variance tighter than a single-band 6E connection. The Bluetooth 5.4 stack supports LC3 for LE Audio devices, but aptX Lossless and LDAC depend on driver-level implementation — MSI has not historically been forthcoming about codec support in its adapter documentation, so verify before committing if audio codec fidelity matters. At this price, the primary trade-off versus a $120+ Intel BE200 M.2 card is likely driver maturity and MLO stability; budget 802.11be silicon occasionally ships with immature firmware that requires updates before MLO behaves reliably. Thermal behavior in a PCIe slot is a non-issue at these power envelopes.\n\nThe right buyer here is a desktop user on a Wi-Fi 7 router who wants to retire a Wi-Fi 5 or Wi-Fi 6 card without committing serious money to the upgrade. Gamers who run wired when possible but need wireless fallback will find the MLO latency reduction meaningful. The HERALD BE9400 competes directly with the TP-Link Archer TXE75E and Asus PCE-BE92BT at similar price points — where it wins or loses will come down to driver polish and MLO implementation quality rather than raw spec differentiation, which is essentially identical across the tier. Skip it in favor of the Intel BE200 if M.2 slot availability is not a constraint and driver ecosystem maturity is the priority. At $49.99 with full Wi-Fi 7 tri-band specs, the value floor is strong; the ceiling depends entirely on firmware execution.",
    price: 49.99,
    category: "Everyday Carry & Accessories",
    categorySlug: "everyday-carry",
