    id: 146,
    title: "Keychron G3 Ultra Light Wireless Mouse",
    slug: "keychron-g3-ultra-light-wireless-mouse",
    description: "At 44 grams, the G3 Ultra sits in the upper tier of ultralight wireless mice — a category currently defined by the Lamzu Atlantis Mini, Logitech G Pro X Superlight 2, and Razer Viper V3 HyperSpeed. Keychron, better known for its mechanical keyboards, is making a deliberate push into competitive peripherals here, and the G3 Ultra is the statement product for that effort. The 44g figure puts it meaningfully lighter than the Superlight 2 DEX (60g) and within a few grams of the Atlantis Mini Pro (48g) and the original Superlight 2 (61g). That weight class signals a specific design philosophy: every structural decision — shell geometry, switch selection, PCB layout — is subordinated to mass reduction.\n\nThe PixArt 3950 is the current reference-class sensor, the same unit found in the G Pro X Superlight 2 and the Viper V3 Pro. It tracks reliably at 30,000 DPI with near-zero jitter at high velocity and a motion sync feature that reduces aliasing at mismatched polling rates. The 8,000 Hz polling rate over 2.4 GHz is where the G3 Ultra makes its clearest competitive argument: at 8K Hz, input latency is theoretically sub-0.125ms per report, which measurably reduces perceived cursor lag versus the 4K Hz ceiling on many competitors. Lift-off distance adjustability down to 0.7mm is noteworthy — that's effectively on-surface tracking termination that will satisfy low-sens players who drag-click or frequently reposition. The three-tier LOD setting (0.7 / 1.0 / 2.0mm) is hardware-configurable rather than software-only, which matters for portability.\n\nBattery runtime splits sharply based on polling rate: 37 hours at 8K Hz is competitive and should cover several extended sessions before a charge is needed, while the 160-hour figure at 125 Hz Bluetooth reflects near-zero polling overhead and makes it a legitimate travel mouse for non-gaming use. Bluetooth 5.3 caps at 125 Hz, so it's a convenience mode only — latency-sensitive use requires the 2.4 GHz dongle. The wired USB option adds zero-latency backup. What's absent is meaningful: no onboard weight adjustment, no tilt-wheel, and Bluetooth polling is non-negotiable at the low end, which will frustrate users who want BT at even 500 Hz. Thermal behavior is largely a non-issue at this weight and power profile. The lack of a charging dock (compared to the Superlight 2's optional PowerPlay ecosystem) is a practical note for buyers who hate cable charging.\n\nThe G3 Ultra targets competitive FPS and Battle Royale players who've already optimized their setup to the gram and want flagship sensor performance without paying the Logitech or Razer brand premium. At $109.99, it undercuts the G Pro X Superlight 2 ($159) and the Viper V3 Pro ($149) while matching them sensor-for-sensor on the 3950. The right buyer is someone running 400–800 DPI with aggressive reposition habits who will immediately notice the LOD adjustment. Casual or productivity-first users should skip this — the 160-hour BT runtime is appealing, but the 125 Hz BT ceiling and the price both argue against it. Someone wanting sub-40g should look at the Lamzu Atlantis or the Finalmouse Ultralight X instead.",
