    id: 149,
    title: "Sony BRAVIA Theatre Bar 5 Soundbar",
    slug: "sony-bravia-theatre-bar-5-soundbar",
    description: "The HT-B500 sits in the increasingly crowded $300–$400 single-bar segment — a price tier where Sonos, Samsung, and Vizio are all fighting hard. Sony slots it as a 3.1-channel system, which immediately distinguishes it from the 2.1 and 2.0 configurations that dominate entry-level. The wireless subwoofer is included in that price, which matters: competitors like the Sonos Ray at $279 ship without a sub entirely, and adding one costs another $749. For the money, the package hardware is reasonably competitive on paper, though the bar's physical driver complement — not yet fully published — will define where the ceiling actually is.\n\nThe 3.1-channel layout means there are dedicated center-channel drivers alongside left and right arrays, which provides meaningful dialogue separation versus pseudo-stereo bars. The declared Dolby Atmos and DTS:X support comes via Sony's Vertical Surround Engine and S-Force PRO Front Surround, both of which are DSP-based spatial processing rather than physical upward-firing drivers. That distinction matters: Atmos height cues here are synthesized, not bounced off your ceiling like they are on bars with discrete up-firing tweeters. The wireless subwoofer handles low-frequency extension, relieving the main bar of bass duties and letting the front drivers focus on mid-range clarity and surround processing. Connectivity is HDMI with eARC capability implied at this tier, plus Bluetooth — notably absent are Wi-Fi streaming and optical input, which the spec sheet doesn't surface. Control runs through the BRAVIA Connect app, Sony's ecosystem management layer with EQ and sound mode access.\n\nIn daily use, the sub-satellite wireless pairing should deliver a cross-over point somewhere around 80–120Hz if Sony holds to its typical tuning, and dropout risk on wireless subs at this price tier is real — worth monitoring across room layouts. Bluetooth connectivity without aptX or LDAC confirmation means codec quality at the bar side is uncertain; expect SBC fallback unless Sony specifies AAC support for Apple device users. The absence of Wi-Fi rules out AirPlay 2, Spotify Connect, and multi-room audio without workarounds — meaningful omissions for anyone expecting a streaming hub, not just a TV companion. HDMI eARC, if present, handles lossless Atmos passthrough from compatible TVs. The DSP-driven height processing performs credibly in rooms under 300 square feet but doesn't produce the discrete vertical layering of bars with physical height drivers like the Sonos Arc or Samsung HW-Q990D.\n\nThe right buyer here is a TV-first listener upgrading from built-in speakers in a smaller living room or bedroom setup, particularly one already inside the Sony BRAVIA TV ecosystem where BRAVIA Connect simplifies integration. The wireless sub inclusion is the defining value argument — at $349.99 all-in, it undercuts comparably configured systems significantly. Someone who streams music as a primary use case and expects a wireless network speaker should look elsewhere, specifically at the Sonos Beam Gen 2 plus Era Sub pairing, which costs considerably more but delivers Wi-Fi audio and a more mature app. The step up within Sony's own line to the HT-A3000 or HT-A5000 brings physical height drivers and multi-room support — worth it for dedicated home theater builds. The HT-B500 wins on value when the brief is purely TV audio with punchy bass, not audiophile streaming.",
