    title: "TESSAN PD 65W USB C Charging Station CS07",
    slug: "tessan-pd-65w-usb-c-charging-station-cs07",
    description: "The CS07 sits in the mid-tier desktop charging station segment — a category crowded by Anker's 727 and Baseus's GaN2 Pro lineup — but targets the 65W GaN sweet spot where most productivity users actually live. The move to a GaN (Gallium Nitride) switching topology is the headline here: it's the reason a unit pushing 65W total output stays compact enough to count as a desk accessory rather than a brick. Four ports total, split evenly between USB-C and USB-A, is a deliberate layout aimed at mixed-device households that haven't fully abandoned Type-A accessories.\n\nThe 65W ceiling is the spec that needs the most unpacking. In a single-port scenario, the primary USB-C port likely delivers a full 65W — enough to charge a 13\" or 14\" MacBook at roughly 70–80% of Apple's own charger speed, or keep a Dell XPS 13 topped up under moderate load. With two USB-C ports active simultaneously, expect power negotiation to drop each port, typically to a 45W / 20W or 33W / 33W split depending on PD 3.0 negotiation — TESSAN hasn't published the exact power-share table, which is a transparency gap worth noting. The two USB-A ports handle 5V/2.4A (12W) apiece, appropriate for headphones, Kindles, and older accessories. GaN switching reduces heat generation relative to silicon-based competitors at this wattage tier, which matters for a unit that's meant to sit permanently on a desk. The 5-foot non-removable cord is long enough for most desk configurations, though the fixed cable is a durability variable — if the cord goes, the unit goes with it.\n\nIn daily use, the realistic scenario is a laptop on one USB-C port and a phone or tablet on the second, with two USB-A slots handling slower-draw peripherals. At that split, the laptop sees adequate but not peak wattage, and phone charging won't hit Qualcomm Quick Charge 3.0 or higher proprietary speeds unless PD negotiation aligns — LDAC, aptX Lossless, and similar codec concerns are off the table here, but the absence of QC 4+ certification means Android flagships relying on Qualcomm's protocol won't hit their rated fast-charge ceilings. Wi-Fi and Bluetooth are irrelevant to the category. Thermal behavior under full four-port load should be manageable given GaN architecture, though the CS07 hasn't been independently tested against the Anker 727's well-documented thermal profile, which remains the class reference at this wattage tier. Value lands cleanly at $69.99 against a 65W output and four-port layout.\n\nThe right buyer is someone running two laptops or a laptop-plus-tablet at a fixed desk who wants to reduce wall-adapter sprawl without stepping up to a 100W+ station. It competes directly with the Anker 727 ($65–75 depending on sale) and undercuts the Ugreen 100W Nexode at that price if 65W is genuinely sufficient. Anyone regularly charging a 16\" MacBook Pro under sustained load should look at 100W-capable alternatives — the CS07's ceiling won't sustain that machine under CPU-intensive work. For the target user — a 13\" or 14\" laptop owner with a mixed USB-C/USB-A device ecosystem — the four-port layout and GaN efficiency make this a rational single-purchase answer to desk clutter.",
    price: 69.99,
    category: "Everyday Carry & Accessories",
