    id: 160,
    title: "Amazon eero Signal 4G LTE Cellular Backup",
    slug: "amazon-eero-signal-4g-lte-cellular-backup",
    description: "The eero Signal lands in a narrow but increasingly relevant product category: dedicated cellular backup units for home mesh networks. This isn't a router with a SIM slot bolted on — it's a standalone failover device designed to pair specifically with eero's mesh ecosystem, sitting alongside products like the GL.iNet Spitz AX and Peplink MAX BR1 Mini in the \"keep the house online when the ISP fails\" drawer. At $99.99 it's priced well below those enterprise-adjacent options, which signals a deliberate push toward mainstream households rather than IT-literate prosumers. The hardware choice here is 4G LTE rather than 5G, which is a conscious positioning decision worth unpacking before anything else.\n\nThe 4G LTE ceiling is the defining technical constraint. Cat-18 LTE theoretical maximums sit around 1.2 Gbps in ideal band aggregation conditions, but real-world residential LTE backup scenarios — where you're riding a carrier's congested mid-band spectrum during a regional outage — will deliver more like 20–80 Mbps down depending on tower load and signal quality. That's enough for video calls, streaming at 1080p, and light work traffic, which is precisely the failover use case. The eSIM implementation matters here: no physical SIM tray means the carrier relationship is entirely software-managed, which is clean for setup but limits you to whatever carrier partnerships Amazon has negotiated at activation time. A separate data subscription is required on top of the hardware cost — the $99.99 buys the radio, not the airtime. eero's tight ecosystem integration implies the failover handoff is handled at the mesh firmware level rather than requiring manual intervention, which is where the value proposition over a generic LTE router actually lives.\n\nIn daily use, the critical metric is failover latency — how many seconds of dropped connectivity occur before the Signal cuts in. eero hasn't published a specific figure, but mesh-integrated failover systems typically target sub-30-second switchover; a generic USB LTE dongle approach can take 2–5 minutes. Sustained throughput on LTE backup will hit a hard ceiling during peak hours on crowded towers, and 4G has no mmWave fallback for dense urban environments where 5G SA coverage might offer relief. There's no Wi-Fi 6E or 6 radio in the Signal itself — it's a WAN uplink device, so all client traffic still flows through the existing eero mesh. The data subscription cost structure is the real ongoing variable: without knowing Amazon's MVNO pricing or which carriers are supported at launch, total cost of ownership over 12 months is genuinely unclear, which is an honest concern for budget-conscious buyers.\n\nThe right buyer here is a remote worker or small-business owner who runs an eero mesh system, has experienced ISP outages that cost real productivity, and wants a low-friction insurance policy rather than a configurable enterprise failover rig. Compared to adding a Nighthawk M6 hotspot and manually switching your eero WAN input, the integrated approach justifies the price. Compared to a Peplink or GL.iNet unit with dual-WAN load balancing, it's significantly simpler but significantly less capable. Skip it if the household already runs a mesh system from another vendor — the eero-specific integration is the core of the value here, and without that ecosystem tie-in, a carrier hotspot does the same job for less.",
    price: 99.99,
    category: "Everyday Carry & Accessories",
    categorySlug: "everyday-carry",
    image: "/images/mirrored/Amazon-eero-Signal-01-1.jpg",
