    id: 167,
    title: "Amazon Blink Mini 2",
    slug: "amazon-blink-mini-2",
    description: "The Blink Mini 2 sits at the entry tier of the plug-in indoor/outdoor security camera market, competing directly with the Wyze Cam v4 and the base Eufy Indoor Cam 2K rather than the PoE or battery-powered mid-range. At $39.99, it undercuts most of its feature-comparable rivals while remaining tethered to Amazon's subscription ecosystem for anything beyond basic live view. The hardware choice to keep this plug-in rather than battery-operated is a deliberate cost and reliability trade-off — continuous power means no compromises on always-on motion detection or extended video sessions, but placement is constrained to outlet proximity.\n\nThe 143° diagonal field of view is meaningfully wide for a fixed-mount camera at this price point — the Wyze Cam v4 covers 122° by comparison, so the Mini 2 captures noticeably more horizontal coverage in a corner-mounted configuration without requiring manual pan adjustment. The 640×360 photo resolution figure is the still-capture ceiling, which is low by 2024 standards and will produce visible blocking artifacts when zooming into details. Video resolution is not explicitly specified in the available data, but Blink's architecture historically delivers 1080p at up to 30fps for video streams — a meaningful distinction from the still-resolution figure. The sensor is a standard fixed-focus CMOS without optical zoom, so effective detail resolution depends heavily on placement distance. Night vision is infrared-based, standard for the category. The IP64 weather-resistance rating on the Mini 2 (versus the Mini 1's indoor-only design) is the generation's headline hardware upgrade, enabling eave mounting and covered outdoor use cases.\n\nIn daily use, the Blink Mini 2's practical performance is shaped more by its cloud dependency than its optics. Without an active Blink Subscription Plan ($3/month per camera or $10/month for unlimited), local storage requires a separately purchased Sync Module 2 and USB drive — a hidden cost that matters at this price tier. Motion detection latency on Blink's platform runs 2–5 seconds to push notification, which trails the Eufy local-processing pipeline by a meaningful margin. Wi-Fi connectivity is 2.4GHz only, which limits throughput headroom and can cause congestion in dense network environments. There is no local RTSP stream natively supported, which will frustrate homelab users. The plug-in design means power draw is continuous at roughly 2–3W, negligible in electricity cost but a consideration in outlet placement. Audio is two-way with a built-in mic and speaker, functional but not optimized for low-noise monitoring.\n\nThe right buyer here is someone already committed to the Alexa ecosystem — Alexa Show display integration is the strongest differentiator versus category-equivalent Wyze hardware. It works well as a porch or garage secondary camera where 1080p is sufficient and cloud latency is acceptable. Buyers who want local processing, RTSP support, or 2K+ resolution should step up to the Eufy Indoor Cam 2K Pro or Reolink E1 Zoom, both available near the $40–50 range. The Mini 2 beats the Wyze Cam v4 on field of view and weather resistance but loses on local storage accessibility and resolution ceiling. At $39.99 without a subscription factored in, value is competitive; with one, the total cost of ownership math changes meaningfully within 12 months.",
    price: 39.99,
    category: "Everyday Carry & Accessories",
    categorySlug: "everyday-carry",
    image: "/images/mirrored/Amazon-Blink-Mini-2-Smart-Security-Camera-01.jpg",
    images: ["/images/mirrored/Amazon-Blink-Mini-2-Smart-Security-Camera-01.jpg", "/images/mirrored/Amazon-Blink-Mini-2-Smart-Security-Camera-Thumbnail-01.jpg", "/images/mirrored/Amazon-Blink-Mini-2-Smart-Security-Camera-02.jpg", "/images/mirrored/Amazon-Blink-Mini-2-Smart-Security-Camera-03.jpg", "/images/mirrored/Amazon-Blink-Mini-2-Smart-Security-Camera-04.jpg"],
