    id: 168,
    title: "Amazon Basics Fire Resistant Security Safe with Programmable Electonic Keyboard",
    slug: "amazon-basics-fire-resistant-security-safe-with-programmable-electonic-keyboard",
    description: "The fire-resistant floor safe market splits cleanly between consumer-grade document boxes and serious security enclosures. At 2.1 cubic feet, this unit lands in the mid-tier residential segment — larger than a typical bedside quick-access pistol safe, smaller than a full-height standalone vault. The alloy steel construction and electronic keypad lock put it squarely against the SentrySafe SFW205GPC and Honeywell 1114 in the $150–$250 bracket. At $218.99, it's positioned as an Amazon Basics value play in a category where brand reputation (Sentry, First Alert, Gardall) normally commands a premium.\n\nAlloy steel construction at this price point typically means 12–14 gauge body panels with a reinforced door — meaningful pry resistance, though not UL Residential Security Container certified territory. The 2.1 cu. ft. interior translates to usable space for standard letter-size documents, passports, hard drives, and small valuables simultaneously — a practical size that competes well against the SentrySafe SFW205, which offers comparable volume. The programmable electronic keypad supports user-defined PIN codes, which matters operationally: you're not dependent on a factory combination or a single override key the way older mechanical locks work. Most units in this class use a motor-driven deadbolt mechanism with a backup physical key override, typically rated for tens of thousands of actuations before service. Fire resistance at this tier is usually rated to 30–60 minutes at 1,200°F — the spec sheet doesn't publish an explicit UL 72 Class rating here, so prospective buyers should confirm whether it carries an independent certification or a manufacturer-only claim, which is a meaningful distinction.\n\nIn daily use, the electronic lock's response time — typically under two seconds from PIN entry to bolt retraction on comparable units — matters more than it sounds when you're retrieving documents under stress. Battery life on keypad safes of this type runs 12–18 months on a standard AA or 9V configuration before low-battery warnings trigger; the backup key is the contingency but should be stored somewhere genuinely separate from the safe. The alloy steel body will conduct heat in a house fire, so paper documents benefit from being stored in fire-resistant envelopes or folders as a secondary layer. There is no Bluetooth or app connectivity here — no audit log, no remote alerts, none of the features appearing on Fort Knox or Viking Security units at $300+. That's a deliberate trade-off for simplicity and lower failure-mode risk.\n\nThe right buyer is someone protecting documents, backup drives, and personal valuables in a home office or bedroom — not a gun owner needing biometric quick access, and not a small business operator needing a UL-listed TL-rated vault. Versus the SentrySafe SFW205GPC at a similar price, the chief question is certification transparency and brand service network, where SentrySafe has a documented edge. The Amazon Basics branding cuts cost but also means a thinner support infrastructure if the lock mechanism fails post-warranty. Skip this for the First Alert 2096DF if an independent fire rating is non-negotiable; consider it if interior volume per dollar is the primary criterion and the certification ambiguity is acceptable.",
    price: 218.99,
    category: "Gaming & Accessories",
    categorySlug: "gaming",
    image: "/images/mirrored/Amazon-Basics-Fire-Resistant-Security-Safe-with-Keypad-01.jpg",
    images: ["/images/mirrored/Amazon-Basics-Fire-Resistant-Security-Safe-with-Keypad-01.jpg", "/images/mirrored/Amazon-Basics-Fire-Resistant-Security-Safe-with-Keypad-02.jpg", "/images/mirrored/Amazon-Basics-Fire-Resistant-Security-Safe-with-Keypad-03.jpg", "/images/mirrored/Amazon-Basics-Fire-Resistant-Security-Safe-with-Keypad-04.jpg"],
    rating: 4.5,
