    id: 201,
    title: "Amazon eero PoE 6 dual-band Wi-Fi 6 access point has a ceiling- or wall-mountable design",
    slug: "amazon-eero-poe-6-dual-band-wi-fi-6-access-point-has-a-ceiling-or-wall-mountable",
    description: "The eero PoE 6 lands in the enterprise-lite / prosumer ceiling-mount access point category, sitting alongside hardware like the TP-Link EAP670 and Ubiquiti UniFi U6 Lite rather than consumer mesh nodes you tuck behind a TV. The defining hardware choice here is Power over Ethernet delivery — no wall-wart, no separate injector required if your switch already supports 802.3af/at — paired with a physical mounting geometry borrowed straight from commercial deployments. At $299.99, it's priced above the EAP670 ($100 street) and competitive with the UniFi U6 Lite, but it's buying into the eero mesh ecosystem rather than a standalone controller stack.\n\nDual-band Wi-Fi 6 with two discrete radios means the PoE 6 runs simultaneous 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz without the band-steering compromises common in tri-band designs — the 2.4 GHz radio handles range and legacy IoT penetration while 5 GHz carries the throughput load. The 2x2 MU-MIMO configuration on each radio caps spatial streams at 4 total, which is a meaningful ceiling: a 4x4 client like a MacBook Pro won't reach its theoretical link rate here, but virtually all phones and tablets are 2x2 or lower, so real-world client saturation is rarely the bottleneck. Support for 160 MHz channel width on 5 GHz enables the higher PHY rates (up to 1,201 Mbps under ideal conditions) that 80 MHz-capped designs can't reach. The ceiling-mount form factor optimizes for omnidirectional coverage in open-plan environments, and at 473g it's solidly built — expect a steel backplate for the PoE mounting bracket.\n\nIn daily deployment, the PoE integration is the primary operational advantage: a single Cat5e/Cat6 run handles both data and power, simplifying ceiling installs where running separate power is expensive. eero's mesh fabric handles roaming via 802.11r/k/v, so client handoff between APs is fast enough to matter for VoIP and video calls in motion. The trade-offs are real, though. Wi-Fi 6 (802.11ax) caps out before Wi-Fi 6E or Wi-Fi 7, so the 6 GHz band is absent — in dense RF environments with significant 5 GHz congestion, a UniFi U6 Pro or TP-Link EAP683 UD gets you that extra spectrum headroom. Bluetooth codec support isn't relevant here. The eero app ecosystem also means network control is cloud-dependent, which is a non-starter for air-gapped or privacy-first deployments that tools like OpenWrt-compatible hardware would suit better.\n\nThe right buyer is a homeowner or small-business operator who wants structured, PoE-based Wi-Fi infrastructure with the eero mesh management layer rather than a VLAN-heavy controller GUI. It makes particular sense in new construction or renovation where Ethernet runs are going in anyway and ceiling mounts are the cleaner choice aesthetically and for coverage geometry. Skip it if the network already runs UniFi — the eero ecosystem won't integrate cleanly. At $299.99, the premium over the EAP670 is essentially the cost of the eero software stack and brand support experience; the underlying Wi-Fi 6 performance envelope isn't meaningfully different from competitors at half the price.",
    price: 299.99,
    category: "Everyday Carry & Accessories",
    categorySlug: "everyday-carry",
    image: "/images/mirrored/Amazon-eero-PoE-6-dual-band-WiFi-6-access-point-01.jpg",
