    id: 202,
    title: "Blink Wired Floodlight Camera boasts 2,600 lumens of brightness and HD live view",
    slug: "blink-wired-floodlight-camera-boasts-2-600-lumens-of-brightness-and-hd-live-view",
    description: "The Blink Wired Floodlight Camera sits in the powered outdoor security camera segment, competing directly with the Ring Floodlight Cam Wired Plus and Eufy Floodlight Cam E340. Unlike battery-dependent floodlight options — Blink's own battery-powered variant included — the wired configuration here means continuous operation without capacity trade-offs or recharge scheduling. The 2,600-lumen output is the headline hardware differentiator: that's meaningfully brighter than the Ring Floodlight Cam Wired Plus at 1,800 lumens and comparable to Eufy's dual-light E340, putting it at the upper end of the consumer floodlight tier without stepping into commercial-grade territory.\n\nThe 1080p sensor resolves at 30 fps with a 143° diagonal field of view — wide enough to cover a full driveway or garage approach in a single frame without the barrel distortion that plagues cheaper ultra-wide optics. At 1080p/30, there's no HDR or high frame rate mode, which is a ceiling that competing units like the Eufy E340 push past with 2K resolution. The 143° diagonal translates to a horizontal angle of roughly 110–115°, which is genuinely useful coverage but means a single unit will leave blind spots on wide property perimeters. The camera integrates natively into Alexa's ecosystem — live view pulls directly into Echo Show devices without a hub, and Alexa Guard compatibility means motion events can feed into broader smart home automations. There's no mention of on-device or local storage; Blink's architecture routes footage through cloud subscription tiers, which is a cost consideration at the $99.99 entry price.\n\nIn daily operation, the wired power source eliminates the anxiety around battery management and ensures the motion-triggered floodlight and camera are always armed. Latency on live view via the Blink app runs 3–5 seconds on a stable Wi-Fi connection — acceptable for monitoring, not useful for real-time security response. The unit operates on 2.4GHz Wi-Fi only; there is no 5GHz band support listed, which will matter in dense RF environments where 2.4GHz congestion is a real problem. Motion detection is PIR-based rather than AI-driven pixel analysis, so expect occasional false triggers from foliage or passing vehicles without zone tuning. Night vision performance is enhanced by the 2,600-lumen output rather than IR illumination alone, which produces more natural-looking color footage in darkness than IR-only systems — a meaningful perceptual advantage over lower-lumen competitors.\n\nThe right buyer here is a homeowner already invested in the Alexa ecosystem who wants continuous outdoor coverage without ongoing battery maintenance and values raw light output over resolution ceiling. Against the Ring Floodlight Cam Wired Plus at a similar price, the Blink wins on lumens and loses on AI motion detection maturity and resolution. Skip the Blink for the Eufy E340 if local storage and 2K resolution are non-negotiable. At $99.99 before any Blink cloud subscription, the hardware cost is competitive, but total cost of ownership climbs with recurring storage fees — factor that in before the Ring ecosystem starts looking more cost-equivalent.",
    price: 99.99,
    category: "Audio & Sound",
    categorySlug: "audio",
    image: "/images/mirrored/Blink-Wired-Floodlight-Camera-Home-Security-Cam-01.jpg",
    images: ["/images/mirrored/Blink-Wired-Floodlight-Camera-Home-Security-Cam-01.jpg", "/images/mirrored/Blink-Wired-Floodlight-Camera-Home-Security-Cam-02.jpg", "/images/mirrored/Blink-Wired-Floodlight-Camera-Home-Security-Cam-03.jpg", "/images/mirrored/Blink-Wired-Floodlight-Camera-Home-Security-Cam-04.jpg"],
