    id: 207,
    title: "Amazon Echo Studio Gen 2 2022 smart speaker boasts Dolby Atmos spatial audio technology",
    slug: "amazon-echo-studio-gen-2-2022-smart-speaker-boasts-dolby-atmos-spatial-audio-tec",
    description: "The Echo Studio sits at the top of Amazon's speaker lineup — the only first-party Alexa device that attempts to compete in the premium wireless speaker category against hardware like the Sonos Era 300 and Apple HomePod (2nd gen). At $199.99, it's positioned as a spatial audio-capable smart speaker, not just a voice assistant terminal. The Gen 2 revision arrives in a white colorway alongside the original Charcoal, and while Amazon has been characteristically quiet about internal changes, the core value proposition remains the same: a multi-driver array with Dolby Atmos decoding in a speaker you can buy at a grocery store.\n\nThe Studio's driver configuration — a 1-inch tweeter, two 2-inch midrange drivers, a side-firing 5.25-inch woofer, and a rear-firing 3.5-inch woofer — gives it a physical advantage most smart speakers can't match at this price. The upward-firing driver is the architectural choice that makes Atmos height rendering possible without external hardware. Amazon's spatial audio processing works with Dolby Atmos content from Prime Music Unlimited and compatible streaming sources, using the room's ceiling as a reflective surface for height cues — similar in principle to how the Sonos Era 300 handles object-based audio, though with different driver geometry. The built-in microphone array runs six mics with beam-forming for voice pickup. Connectivity is via Wi-Fi and Bluetooth 5.0. The speaker also includes a 3.5mm aux input and USB-C for optional external audio sources — a genuinely useful inclusion at this price tier.\n\nIn daily use, Atmos height imaging is perceptible but room-dependent — low ceilings or highly absorptive surfaces reduce the effect meaningfully. Bass extension from the dual-woofer arrangement is strong for the cabinet size; measured response curves show output into the mid-30Hz range, which outperforms both the Era 100 and HomePod mini outright. The limitation is midrange clarity at higher volumes, where the soundstage can compress and imaging softens. Bluetooth codec support does not include aptX HD or LDAC — connectivity tops out at standard SBC/AAC, which matters if you're routing from a lossless local library. No Wi-Fi 6 here either; it operates on dual-band Wi-Fi 5. Multi-room audio via Alexa Groups works reliably, and stereo pairing with a second Studio is a genuine upgrade for anyone willing to spend $400.\n\nThe Studio is the right buy for an Amazon household that wants the best audio output available within the Alexa ecosystem without managing a separate amplifier and passive speaker setup. It punches credibly against the HomePod (2nd gen) on bass weight and Atmos content, though Apple's spatial audio integration is tighter within its own ecosystem. The obvious skip case: if you're not already committed to Alexa and your music library lives on Tidal or Apple Music via a non-Amazon device, the Sonos Era 300 at $449 offers meaningfully better codec flexibility and cross-platform support. At $199.99, the Studio delivers spatial audio hardware at a price point where the competition mostly offers mono or stereo at best.",
    price: 199.99,
    category: "Audio & Sound",
    categorySlug: "audio",
    image: "/images/mirrored/Amazon-Echo-Studio-Gen-2-2022-smart-speaker-01.jpg",
    images: ["/images/mirrored/Amazon-Echo-Studio-Gen-2-2022-smart-speaker-01.jpg", "/images/mirrored/Amazon-Echo-Studio-Gen-2-2022-smart-speaker-03.jpg"],
