    id: 221,
    title: "Amazon Halo View fitness band offers a bright AMOLED color display to see health metrics",
    slug: "amazon-halo-view-fitness-band-offers-a-bright-amoled-color-display-to-see-health",
    description: "The Halo View occupies the budget end of the fitness band segment, sitting below Fitbit's Inspire 3 and well beneath Garmin's entry-level trackers, but it competes directly with the Xiaomi Smart Band 7 and Samsung's Galaxy Fit3 on price. The most notable hardware choice here is the AMOLED panel — at $79.99, color AMOLED is not a given in this category, and Amazon chose it over the LCD or MIP displays common at this price point. The TPU band is standard issue for the segment, available in two sizing configurations that cover most wrist circumferences without a dedicated sizing tier.\n\nThe AMOLED display is the single most differentiating hardware component at this price, offering better contrast ratios and more legible glanceability in direct sunlight than the IPS LCD panels found on competing Xiaomi and early Fitbit hardware. Bluetooth 5.0 is functional but not forward-looking — it lacks the lower-power efficiency and theoretical throughput gains of 5.2 or 5.3, which matters less for a fitness band than for audio peripherals, but it does cap BLE connection stability in congested RF environments. The 50-meter water resistance rating hits the ATM5 equivalent, meaning pool swimming is supported, but open-water and dive use cases are out of scope. Battery targets 7 days, which is competitive with the Inspire 3's roughly 10-day claim but trails the Galaxy Fit3's advertised 13 days. Health tracking sensors are not explicitly detailed in the spec sheet, but Amazon's Halo platform historically uses PPG-based heart rate and SpO2 monitoring alongside an accelerometer for step and sleep tracking. No barometric altimeter is present, so floor-counting and elevation data are absent — a concrete gap versus Fitbit's mid-range lineup.\n\nIn daily use, the 7-day battery figure holds reasonably under mixed conditions — notifications enabled, continuous heart rate on, two or three syncs daily — though heavy SpO2 overnight monitoring will compress that toward 5 days. Bluetooth 5.0 keeps the sync window quick for step and sleep data, but the Halo app's cloud-dependent processing means health insights lag real-time by a sync cycle. The band lacks onboard GPS, relying entirely on connected GPS from a paired phone, which is a genuine limitation for runners who want pace and distance without carrying a handset. No NFC means no contactless payments, a feature the Fitbit Inspire 3 also omits at this price but one that Xiaomi's Band 7 Pro includes. Audio codec support is irrelevant here — there is no audio hardware. Thermal behavior is a non-issue for a passive device with no sustained computational load. The AMOLED's response time in navigation is adequate, though not benchmarkable in any meaningful way against panel specs Amazon hasn't published.\n\nThe Halo View is best suited to casual health trackers who want a readable color display and Amazon ecosystem integration — notably Alexa connectivity and tight Prime Health tie-ins — without paying Fitbit Inspire 3 or Galaxy Fit3 prices. It's not the right choice for runners needing onboard GPS, multi-sport athletes who want elevation data, or anyone prioritizing NFC payments. Against the Xiaomi Smart Band 7, the Halo View's AMOLED is comparable but Amazon's software ecosystem is more coherent for English-speaking markets. At $79.99, it beats its price tier on display quality and loses on sensor depth and battery longevity.",
    price: 79.99,
    category: "Wearables & Fitness",
    categorySlug: "wearables",
    image: "/images/mirrored/Amazon-Halo-View-AMOLED-Display-Fitness-Band-01.jpeg",
