    id: 237,
    title: "Amazon Echo Show 10 (3rd Gen) smart display has a motorized base",
    slug: "amazon-echo-show-10-3rd-gen-smart-display-has-a-motorized-base",
    description: "The Echo Show 10 occupies the premium tier of Amazon's smart display lineup — above the Show 8 and well above the Show 5 — and its defining hardware differentiator is a 360-degree motorized base that physically rotates the unit to track the user around the room. That mechanic puts it in a genuinely distinct product category from static smart displays like the Google Nest Hub Max or Lenovo Smart Display 13, which rely entirely on wide-angle optics to compensate for fixed orientation. The 10-inch display sits at the top of the Echo Show family's screen size range before you get into the 15-inch wall-mount variant, making it the largest portable unit Amazon produces.\n\nThe 10-inch panel is an IPS LCD — not AMOLED — running at 1280×800 (149 ppi), which is adequate for kitchen-counter or desk distances but won't satisfy anyone scrutinizing fine detail or expecting OLED contrast ratios. The motorized rotation mechanism is driven by an integrated motor with a reported tracking range covering roughly 350 degrees of horizontal sweep, relying on the front camera and on-device processing to detect human presence and follow it without continuous cloud round-trips. The camera itself is a 13MP wide-angle unit, which feeds both the motion-tracking pipeline and video calls over Zoom, Chime, or Alexa's native Drop In. Audio is a 2.1 configuration: two front-firing tweeters paired with a side-firing woofer. Amazon rates this as the loudest Echo Show to date in the portable lineup, though specific SPL figures aren't published. The octa-core MediaTek SoC onboard handles local wake-word detection and the tracking compute simultaneously — Amazon doesn't publish the exact die, but thermal behavior in sustained video call scenarios is generally well-managed at room temperature.\n\nIn daily use, the motor tracking is the feature that either justifies the $250 price or doesn't, and it's genuinely useful during cooking or workouts where the user moves laterally across the room — the display reorients in roughly one to two seconds with minimal overshoot. That said, the mechanism introduces audible motor noise in quiet rooms, which is a real trade-off for a bedside or office deployment. Connectivity is Wi-Fi 5 (802.11a/b/g/n/ac), dual-band, which is a notable step below Wi-Fi 6 at this price point in 2024 — range and throughput in congested 5 GHz environments will occasionally show lag in video streams. Bluetooth is 5.0 with support for A2DP, allowing the unit to function as a Bluetooth speaker, though codec support is limited to SBC — no aptX, LDAC, or AAC. Alexa Guard integration adds passive ambient sound monitoring (smoke alarm, glass break detection) when the household is away, which is a legitimate home-security utility layer on top of the display's core function.\n\nThe right buyer here is a home-kitchen or home-office user who video-calls regularly and wants a display that doesn't require them to stay anchored in one spot — the tracking mechanic delivers real utility in those contexts in a way no fixed smart display can replicate. Against the Nest Hub Max at $229, the Echo Show 10 offers the motion tracking and a stronger speaker system but locks users into Amazon's ecosystem and trails on smart home Matter/Thread integration breadth. Anyone deeply invested in Google Home or Apple HomeKit should look elsewhere. The static Echo Show 8 at $149 undercuts it substantially for users who don't need tracking or the larger screen. At $249, the motor is what the premium buys — and for the right use case, it's worth it.",
    price: 249.99,
    category: "Everyday Carry & Accessories",
    categorySlug: "everyday-carry",
