    id: 257,
    title: "Amazon eero Pro 7: Tri-Band Mesh Wi-Fi 7 Router with Support for Internet Plans up to 5 Gbps",
    slug: "amazon-eero-pro-7-tri-band-mesh-wi-fi-7-router-with-support-for-internet-plans-u",
    description: "Eero's flagship mesh node sits at the top of a crowded Wi-Fi 7 mesh category that includes the Netgear Orbi 970, TP-Link Deco BE95, and Google Nest Wi-Fi Pro. At $299.99 per node (or roughly $550 for a two-pack), the Pro 7 is priced aggressively below the Orbi 970 while targeting the same 5 Gbps WAN headroom that's increasingly relevant as multi-gig ISP tiers roll out to consumers. The tri-band radio configuration — two 6 GHz channels plus one 2.4 GHz — is the meaningful hardware decision here: dedicating one 6 GHz band exclusively to backhaul while the second serves clients is the architecture that separates serious mesh systems from budget compromises.\n\nWi-Fi 7's headline feature is Multi-Link Operation (MLO), and the Pro 7 supports it, meaning compatible clients can aggregate across bands simultaneously rather than committing to one. That matters practically: a Wi-Fi 7 laptop or phone in a congested environment gets lower latency and more consistent throughput rather than just peak bandwidth bragging rights. The tri-band layout enables a dedicated 6 GHz backhaul link that, in clean RF environments, comfortably sustains 2.5–3 Gbps between nodes — enough that the wireless backbone doesn't become the bottleneck on a 2.5 Gbps fiber plan. The 5 Gbps WAN ceiling requires a compatible modem and ISP delivery; most users will cap at 2.5 Gbps via the physical port configuration. Eero's 320 MHz channel support in the upper 6 GHz band also means theoretical aggregate throughput of 6 Gbps or higher on paper, though real-world client-side 4K streams will land around 1.2–1.8 Gbps in typical home deployments.\n\nThe eero app remains one of the more polished mesh management experiences — setup is genuinely fast, roaming handoff is handled via 802.11r/k/v, and the node placement suggestions are calibrated against signal maps. Latency to gateway under load sits in the 2–5ms range for wired backhaul and 8–14ms wirelessly, competitive with Orbi 970 at roughly half the price. The trade-off is eero's software ecosystem: advanced features like VLAN tagging, custom DNS per-device, and full firewall rules require eero Plus at $99/year. WPA3 is standard. Bluetooth and Thread radios are integrated, feeding into Amazon's Matter-over-Thread smart home stack — useful if the household runs Echo devices and Matter sensors. There is no USB port, no local DHCP failover mode, and WAN aggregation isn't supported, which will frustrate power users coming from pfSense or Unifi.\n\nThe right buyer here is a household running 1 Gbps or faster internet with a mix of Wi-Fi 7 and Wi-Fi 6 devices who wants reliable coverage across 3,000–6,000 square feet without managing a prosumer system. It competes directly with the Google Nest Wi-Fi Pro at a lower per-node cost for equivalent 6 GHz backhaul performance, and it edges out TP-Link Deco BE65 on sustained throughput. Skip it if advanced routing control is non-negotiable — a Ubiquiti UniFi setup at similar total cost delivers far more configurability. The embedded Alexa integration and Thread border router add genuine value for Amazon ecosystem households; for everyone else, those are neutral features.",
    price: 299.99,
    category: "Smart Home",
    categorySlug: "smart-home",
    image: "/images/mirrored/Amazon-eero-Pro-7-Tri-Band-Mesh-Wi-Fi-7-Router-01.jpg",
