    id: 260,
    title: "Amazon Echo Dot 4th-Generation smart speaker is smaller and rounder",
    slug: "amazon-echo-dot-4th-generation-smart-speaker-is-smaller-and-rounder",
    description: "The fourth-generation Echo Dot sits at the budget end of Amazon's own speaker lineup — below the Echo (4th-gen) with its 3\" woofer and tweeter array, and well below the Echo Studio's 5.25\" woofer and Dolby Atmos processing. At $59.99, it competes directly with the Google Nest Mini (2nd-gen) and Apple HomePod mini at its floor, though the HomePod mini is nearly double the price. The spherical enclosure is a genuine departure from the puck-era design, and the single 1.6\" full-range driver is the core hardware limitation that defines everything else about the listening experience.\n\nThe 1.6\" driver is a single full-range unit — no dedicated tweeter, no passive radiator — which means the frequency response rolls off steeply below roughly 100Hz and compresses at the top end above 15kHz. Don't expect meaningful bass extension; the bottom octave (20–60Hz) simply isn't there. Four-microphone array is a step up from the prior two-mic configuration, and in practice wake-word detection at 3–5 meters in moderately noisy rooms is reliable. Connectivity is Bluetooth 5.0 without aptX or LDAC support — it's limited to SBC, which caps wireless audio quality at roughly 320kbps effective throughput. The 3.5mm auxiliary output is a significant inclusion at this price, letting it feed a powered monitor or AV receiver and offload audio processing entirely to a better transducer. AirPlay compatibility is present but strictly for audio routing — Siri is not accessible through the hardware.\n\nIn daily use, the four-mic array handles simultaneous playback and wake detection better than the prior generation, though it still struggles when audio exceeds 70dB SPL. The speaker itself peaks around 78dB at 1 meter before distortion becomes audible — adequate for a bedside table or kitchen counter, not a living room. There's no onboard DSP equalization accessible to users, so FR tuning is Amazon-controlled via the app and tends toward a mid-forward signature optimized for voice clarity over music reproduction. Alexa's local voice processing for basic commands (timers, alarms) adds measurable latency reduction. Wi-Fi is dual-band 802.11 a/b/g/n/ac — no Wi-Fi 6. The LED ring clock display on some variants (sold separately as Echo Dot with Clock) is absent here, which matters if the use case is bedside time-checking.\n\nThe right buyer here is someone building an Alexa-centric multi-room audio network on a strict budget, or furnishing a secondary room where audio quality is a low priority relative to voice assistant access. The 3.5mm out makes it a functional smart speaker controller for anyone with a decent passive speaker setup. Skip it if music quality matters — the HomePod mini delivers substantially better audio at $99, and the Echo (4th-gen) at $99 adds a dedicated tweeter and woofer that changes the sonic profile entirely. At $59.99, the Echo Dot beats the Nest Mini on Alexa integration depth and the aux output wins it against most competitors in this tier, but it doesn't beat anything on sound quality.",
    price: 59.99,
    category: "Audio & Sound",
    categorySlug: "audio",
