    id: 270,
    title: "All-New Amazon Echo Fabric Smart Speaker has the hardware you love from the Echo Plus",
    slug: "all-new-amazon-echo-fabric-smart-speaker-has-the-hardware-you-love-from-the-echo",
    description: "The third-generation Echo sits in the mid-tier of Amazon's own speaker lineup — above the Echo Dot's single-driver compromise but below the Echo Studio's 5.1-channel array and Dolby Atmos processing. At $99, it competes directly with the Google Nest Audio and the Apple HomePod mini, and it inherits the internal hardware architecture from the Echo Plus, which previously carried Amazon's best audio components before the Studio absorbed that crown. The cylindrical fabric-wrapped form factor is a deliberate acoustic choice, not just aesthetics — a 360-degree driver arrangement fires outward in all directions, which changes how the speaker interacts with room acoustics compared to a front-facing design.\n\nThe Echo Plus donor hardware matters here because that platform centered on a 2.5-inch woofer paired with a 0.6-inch tweeter in a ported enclosure tuned for a bass response that punched well beyond the cabinet volume — a known strength of that generation. The fabric shell (available in Charcoal, Heather Gray, Sandstone, and Twilight Blue colorways) is acoustically transparent, meaning the enclosure geometry, not the textile, is doing the tonal work. Alexa processing runs on Amazon's own silicon rather than a licensed application processor — latency from wake word to cloud round-trip typically lands under 1.5 seconds on a clean Wi-Fi connection. The device supports Wi-Fi 802.11 a/b/g/n dual-band (2.4 and 5 GHz) and Bluetooth 5.0, but notably caps Bluetooth audio codec support at SBC — no aptX, no LDAC, no AAC — which limits the Echo's usefulness as a wireless Bluetooth speaker from a phone versus its role as an Alexa endpoint.\n\nIn daily use, the 360-degree output makes placement flexible — corners and shelves that would strangle a directional speaker work fine here — but the tuning skews toward mid-bass warmth rather than flat reference. Vocals and acoustic instruments resolve clearly; orchestral dynamics and sub-bass extension are where the Studio's 5.25-inch woofer separates itself. Stereo pairing two Echo units via the Alexa app is supported and does meaningfully widen the soundstage for a dedicated listening room, though channel crosstalk isn't eliminated entirely. The absence of a 3.5mm line-out on this generation is a real omission for users who want to drive external speakers. Multi-room audio via Alexa Groups works reliably over Wi-Fi with sub-200ms sync drift in practice — acceptable for background listening, audible on synchronized playback in adjacent rooms.\n\nThe right buyer is someone already committed to the Alexa ecosystem who wants capable whole-home audio without the $199 Studio in every room. It outperforms the Nest Audio in bass body and matches it in voice recognition accuracy in noisy environments, though Google Assistant's query handling still edges Alexa on complex follow-up questions. The HomePod mini beats it on audio fidelity per dollar if the household runs Apple Music and AirPlay 2, but offers essentially no third-party smart home flexibility by comparison. Skip this for the Echo Studio if the primary use case is critical listening or home theater fill. At $99 — and frequently discounted to $69–$79 — the inherited Plus hardware makes it strong value for ambient listening and smart home control.",
    price: 99,
    category: "Audio & Sound",
